Task 1: Install and Configure Smart Card Middleware
Subtask 1.1: Install Required Packages
First, we need to install the necessary packages for smart card support.

Connect to your lab machine and open a terminal as root user.

Update the system packages:

dnf update -y
Install smart card middleware packages:
dnf install -y pcsc-lite pcsc-lite-libs pcsc-tools opensc coolkey
Install additional authentication packages:
dnf install -y pam_pkcs11 mozilla-pkcs11-loader nss-tools
Verify the installation:
rpm -qa | grep -E "(pcsc|opensc|coolkey|pam_pkcs11)"
Subtask 1.2: Start and Enable Smart Card Services
Start the PC/SC daemon:
systemctl start pcscd
systemctl enable pcscd
Verify the service is running:
systemctl status pcscd
Check for smart card readers (this will show virtual readers in our lab environment):
pcsc_scan
Press Ctrl+C to exit the scan after a few seconds.

Subtask 1.3: Configure OpenSC for Smart Card Access
Create OpenSC configuration directory:
mkdir -p /etc/opensc
Create OpenSC configuration file:
cat > /etc/opensc/opensc.conf << 'EOF'
app default {
    debug = 0;
    debug_file = /tmp/opensc-debug.log;
    
    framework pkcs15 {
        use_file_caching = true;
        pin_cache = true;
        pin_cache_counter = 10;
    }
}
EOF
Set proper permissions:
chmod 644 /etc/opensc/opensc.conf
Task 2: Configure Smart Card Authentication in IdM
Subtask 2.1: Prepare IdM for Smart Card Authentication
Ensure IdM server is running:
systemctl status ipa
Authenticate as IdM admin:
kinit admin
Enter the admin password when prompted.

Check current IdM configuration:
ipa config-show | grep -i cert
Subtask 2.2: Create Certificate Authority for Smart Cards
Create a directory for certificate operations:
mkdir -p /root/smartcard-certs
cd /root/smartcard-certs
Generate a private key for the CA:
openssl genrsa -out ca-key.pem 4096
Create CA certificate:
openssl req -new -x509 -key ca-key.pem -out ca-cert.pem -days 365 -subj "/C=US/ST=State/L=City/O=Lab Organization/OU=IT Department/CN=Smart Card CA"
Import CA certificate into IdM:
ipa-cacert-manage install ca-cert.pem
systemctl restart httpd
Subtask 2.3: Configure Smart Card Authentication Policy
Enable smart card authentication globally:
ipa config-mod --user-auth-type=password --user-auth-type=otp --user-auth-type=pkinit
Create a smart card authentication rule:
ipa krbtpolicy-mod --maxlife=86400 --maxrenew=604800
Configure PAM for smart card authentication:
authselect select ipa with-smartcard --force
Subtask 2.4: Create User Certificates
Create a test user in IdM:
ipa user-add testuser --first=Test --last=User --email=testuser@example.com
Set a temporary password for the user:
echo "TempPass123!" | ipa passwd testuser --password
Generate a private key for the test user:
openssl genrsa -out testuser-key.pem 2048
Create a certificate signing request:
openssl req -new -key testuser-key.pem -out testuser-csr.pem -subj "/C=US/ST=State/L=City/O=Lab Organization/OU=Users/CN=testuser"
Sign the certificate with our CA:
openssl x509 -req -in testuser-csr.pem -CA ca-cert.pem -CAkey ca-key.pem -CAcreateserial -out testuser-cert.pem -days 365
Create PKCS#12 file for the smart card:
openssl pkcs12 -export -out testuser.p12 -inkey testuser-key.pem -in testuser-cert.pem -certfile ca-cert.pem -password pass:smartcard123
Task 3: Test Smart Card Authentication for User Login
Subtask 3.1: Simulate Smart Card with Software Token
Install software token simulator:
dnf install -y softhsm2
Initialize the software token:
softhsm2-util --init-token --slot 0 --label "TestCard" --so-pin 123456 --pin 1234
Import the user certificate into the token:
pkcs11-tool --module /usr/lib64/pkcs11/libsofthsm2.so --login --pin 1234 --write-object testuser-cert.pem --type cert --label "testuser"
Import the private key into the token:
openssl rsa -in testuser-key.pem -out testuser-key-plain.pem
pkcs11-tool --module /usr/lib64/pkcs11/libsofthsm2.so --login --pin 1234 --write-object testuser-key-plain.pem --type privkey --label "testuser"
Subtask 3.2: Configure PAM for Smart Card Authentication
Create PAM PKCS#11 configuration:
cat > /etc/pam_pkcs11/pam_pkcs11.conf << 'EOF'
pam_pkcs11 {
    nullok = false;
    debug = false;
    use_first_pass = false;
    try_first_pass = false;
    use_authtok = false;
    card_only = false;
    wait_for_card = false;
    
    pkcs11_module opensc {
        module = /usr/lib64/pkcs11/libsofthsm2.so;
        description = "SoftHSM PKCS#11 library";
        slot_num = 0;
        support_threads = false;
        ca_dir = /etc/pam_pkcs11/cacerts;
        crl_dir = /etc/pam_pkcs11/crls;
        cert_policy = ca,signature;
    }
    
    mapper ms {
        debug = false;
        module = /usr/lib64/pam_pkcs11/subject_mapping.so;
        ignorecase = false;
        mapfile = /etc/pam_pkcs11/subject_mapping;
    }
}
EOF
Create certificate directories:
mkdir -p /etc/pam_pkcs11/cacerts /etc/pam_pkcs11/crls
Copy CA certificate to PAM directory:
cp ca-cert.pem /etc/pam_pkcs11/cacerts/
Create subject mapping file:
echo "testuser -> testuser" > /etc/pam_pkcs11/subject_mapping
Subtask 3.3: Test Smart Card Authentication
Test PKCS#11 token access:
pkcs11-tool --module /usr/lib64/pkcs11/libsofthsm2.so --list-objects --pin 1234
Test certificate verification:
pkcs11-tool --module /usr/lib64/pkcs11/libsofthsm2.so --list-certificates --pin 1234
Create a test authentication script:
cat > /root/test-smartcard-auth.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
echo "Testing Smart Card Authentication..."

# Test with pklogin_finder
echo "Checking certificate mapping..."
/usr/lib64/pam_pkcs11/pklogin_finder debug

# Test authentication
echo "Testing authentication (this is a simulation)..."
echo "In a real environment, you would:"
echo "1. Insert smart card"
echo "2. Run: ssh -o PreferredAuthentications=publickey testuser@localhost"
echo "3. Enter PIN when prompted"

echo "Smart card authentication test completed."
EOF
Make the script executable and run it:
chmod +x /root/test-smartcard-auth.sh
./test-smartcard-auth.sh
Subtask 3.4: Verify Smart Card Configuration
Check smart card reader status:
opensc-tool --list-readers
Verify certificate chain:
openssl verify -CAfile ca-cert.pem testuser-cert.pem
Test PKCS#11 functionality:
p11tool --list-all --provider=/usr/lib64/pkcs11/libsofthsm2.so
Check PAM configuration:
authselect current
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: PC/SC Daemon Not Starting
Problem: pcscd service fails to start Solution:

# Check for conflicting processes
ps aux | grep pcscd
# Kill any existing processes
pkill pcscd
# Restart the service
systemctl restart pcscd
Issue 2: Certificate Import Fails
Problem: Cannot import certificate to smart card Solution:

# Check token status
softhsm2-util --show-slots
# Reinitialize if necessary
softhsm2-util --init-token --slot 0 --label "TestCard" --so-pin 123456 --pin 1234 --force
Issue 3: Authentication Fails
Problem: Smart card authentication is rejected Solution:

# Check certificate validity
openssl x509 -in testuser-cert.pem -text -noout
# Verify subject mapping
cat /etc/pam_pkcs11/subject_mapping
# Check PAM logs
journalctl -u systemd-logind | tail -20
Verification Steps
Verify middleware installation:
rpm -qa | grep -E "(pcsc|opensc)" | wc -l
This should return a number greater than 0.

Check service status:
systemctl is-active pcscd
This should return "active".

Verify certificate creation:
ls -la /root/smartcard-certs/*.pem | wc -l
This should show multiple certificate files.

Test token functionality:
softhsm2-util --show-slots | grep -c "TestCard"
This should return 1.

Conclusion
In this lab, you have successfully:

• Installed and configured smart card middleware including PC/SC daemon, OpenSC, and related tools • Set up a complete smart card authentication infrastructure in Red Hat Identity Management • Created and managed digital certificates for smart card authentication • Configured PAM (Pluggable Authentication Modules) to support smart card login • Tested smart card authentication using software token simulation

Why This Matters: Smart card authentication provides a significant security enhancement over traditional password-based authentication. By requiring both something you have (the smart card) and something you know (the PIN), it implements true multi-factor authentication. This is crucial for:

• High-security environments where strong authentication is mandatory • Compliance requirements such as FIPS 140-2 or Common Criteria • Enterprise security policies that require multi-factor authentication • Protection against password-based attacks like brute force and credential theft

The skills you've learned in this lab are directly applicable to real-world enterprise environments where smart card authentication is becoming increasingly common for securing access to critical systems and data.

Next Steps: In production environments, you would integrate this with physical smart card readers and actual smart cards, configure certificate enrollment processes, and implement comprehensive certificate lifecycle management.
